Definition 1.1 states that a \(T_0\)-space \(D\) is injective when, whenever \(X\subseteq Y\) as a subspace, every continuous \(f:X\to D\) extends to a continuous map \(Y\to D\). The same note asserts that injectivity with respect to monomorphisms in the category of \(T_0\)-spaces and continuous maps holds only for the one-point space, and it points to Proposition 2.11 and Theorem 2.12 for the identification with continuous lattices. The AI English of `InjectiveSpace` establishes the extension property along topological embeddings: for an embedding of \(X\) into \(Y\) and a continuous map \(X\to D\), some continuous map \(Y\to D\) agrees with it on the image of the embedding (`InjectiveSpace.extension`). That is the subspace formulation of the definition. The one-point monomorphism claim and the citations of 2.11 and 2.12 are absent from the AI English. **Theorem:** partial — the extension clause matches `InjectiveSpace.extension`, and the one-point monomorphism claim is unchecked. **Proof:** no — the note is a definition, and the AI English does not prove the one-point assertion.
